I totally agree with you on this statement. 
I implemented it this way because `sync.Cond` does not have a nice way to interact with selects for multiple channel reads. For instance to run `sync.Cond` with this setup It will look something similar to this:
```go 

	for {
			select {
			// subscription was closed and there is no more interest in the topic
			case <-s.closeC:
				return
				// program exited successefully
			case <-s.execCloseC:
				_ = s.sendPayload(lastPayloadSent, true)
				s.close()
				return
			default:
				// it will block here until call to cond.Broadcast() is done, which means that if we want to cancel this subscription (closing s.closeC) and release only this goroutine we will end up waking every go routine that is waiting on this cond.Wait() in order to clean a single goroutine. The same happens if the program exited but in this case makes more sense to wake up everyone
				s.cond.Wait()
				lastPayloadSent = s.sendPayload(lastPayloadSent, false)
			}

		}
```

`sync.Cond` API will be changed in go "2" in order to support select statements.

`mysync.Cond` was developed to have the same behavior of `sync.Cond` which means that to wake up someone we require a broadcast or it will block forever. It can block if a waiter waits after the last Broadcast happened but that control is done by using an extra channel that is closed once the program exited and we can return and forget about `cond.Wait()`

In the code it will look like this:

```go

switch st:=job.Status.(type) {

case *pb.Job_Running:
// st will be pb.Job_Running{ Running *proto.Empty}
// for now we do not care about the content since it's still running
case *pb.Job_Stopped:
//  st will be *pb.Job_Stopped

}


```
